PG.notes("s09-display-flex", "<p class=\"summary\">Opens the Flexbox section with a history of page-layout techniques (tables, inline-block, absolute positioning, floats) and why flexbox replaced them. Shows that display: flex on a container lays children in a row, introduces gap and inline-flex, then has the learner turn a list into a horizontal nav bar.</p>\n<h3>Layout methods that came before flexbox</h3>\n<ul><li>HTML tables (table, tr, td) with set column widths can build a multi-column layout, but table markup should only be used for real tabular data, never for page layout.</li><li>display: inline-block with explicit widths puts boxes on one line, but vertical alignment is awkward and elements collapse down when space runs out, so it is fiddly to control.</li><li>position: absolute pulls boxes out of normal flow and offsets them (for example with left); it works but is inflexible: anything added later forces re-adjustment and it does not respond to screen size.</li><li>float (popular circa 2005-2010) can make columns by floating items left or right and clearing a footer; it needed workarounds such as the clearfix hack and is hard to reason about and debug.</li><li>Takeaway: keep float for its original job (wrapping text around an image) and use flexbox, grid or a framework for page structure. Floats can still be used inside individual blocks.</li></ul>\n<h3>display: flex basics</h3>\n<ul><li>Wrap the elements in a container element and set display: flex on the container (the parent), not on the children.</li><li>The direct children become flex items and line up in a row, each normally sized by its content, regardless of whether they were block or inline elements before.</li><li>Flexbox is a separate layout system: the usual display rules (div is block, span is inline, etc.) no longer apply to the items; flexbox rules take over. The container itself behaves like a block (full available width).</li><li>gap sets spacing between items. A fixed value such as 10px is static; a rem value such as 1rem scales with the root font size.</li><li>display: inline-flex makes the container itself behave inline (only as wide as needed, other content can share its line), mirroring the block / inline-block distinction. The items inside behave the same either way.</li><li>Many more properties (axes, direction, alignment, wrapping) are covered in later lessons; the next lesson covers the flex axis and direction.</li></ul>\n<h3>Demo files that come with the exercise</h3>\n<ul><li>index.html links to demo pages: html-table.html, inline-block.html, absolute-position.html, float.html and flex.html, each producing a three-column layout so you can compare the code needed. flex.html needs only a few lines.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> In the reference repo the three-column flex demo is just .container { display: flex; gap: 10px; } with the columns as divs inside.</li></ul>\n<h3>Challenge walkthrough</h3>\n<ul><li>Starting page: a div.container holding a paragraph title and several li links, with list-style: none already removing the bullets, so everything stacks vertically.</li><li>Fix: in the .container rule change display to flex, which puts all items on one line like a nav bar.</li><li>Add a gap (for example 10px or 1rem) so the links are not touching.</li><li>The solution therefore only adds two declarations: display: flex and gap.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Flex container with gap</p><pre data-lang=\"css\">.container {\n  display: flex;\n  gap: 10px;   /* or 1rem to scale with root font size */\n}</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Challenge solution: list items become a horizontal nav bar</p><pre data-lang=\"css\">p  { font-weight: 700; margin: 0; }\nli { list-style: none; }\n.container {\n  padding: 10px;\n  background-color: gold;\n  display: flex;\n  gap: 10px;\n}</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">inline-flex variant</p><pre data-lang=\"css\">.container { display: inline-flex; gap: 1rem; }</pre>\n<h2>The task: Turn a list into a horizontal nav bar with flexbox</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> index.html with a div.container containing a title paragraph and five li links (list-style none already applied); items stack vertically. A goal image shows the target.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Display all items on one horizontal line with a gap between them, using flexbox.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Open index.html in preview and compare with goal.png.</li><li>In the .container rule set display: flex.</li><li>Add a gap (10px or a rem value) to space the items.</li><li>Toggle list-style to see what it does, then check solution.html.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Title and links sit in a single gold row separated by even gaps.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"css\">.container {\n  padding: 10px;\n  background-color: gold;\n  display: flex;\n  gap: 10px;\n}</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Setting display: flex on the items instead of the container.</li><li>Expecting old block/inline rules to still apply to flex items.</li><li>Using HTML tables or floats for page layout.</li><li>The reference repo has an author footer ('Made with ... by ...') that is not part of the course; its index.html also already contains the finished flex solution.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("s09-flex-direction", "<p class=\"summary\">Explains flex-direction, the main axis and cross axis, and why properties like flex-basis mean width in a row but height in a column. The exercise stacks a rainbow of divs vertically using inline-flex, flex-direction: column and a child-combinator selector for flex-basis.</p>\n<h3>Default flow versus flex flow</h3>\n<ul><li>Normal HTML flow stacks inline items along a line until space runs out and stacks block items top to bottom.</li><li>Once a container has display: flex, its children line up on a single row, left to right. This comes from the flex-direction property, whose default value is row.</li><li>Think of 'row' as the direction in which the next item is placed, not as the number of rows; a row-direction container visually looks like columns side by side.</li></ul>\n<h3>Main axis and cross axis</h3>\n<ul><li>flex-direction defines the main axis: row gives a horizontal main axis (left to right); column gives a vertical main axis (top to bottom).</li><li>The cross axis is always perpendicular to the main axis: vertical for row, horizontal for column.</li><li>Axes matter because many flex properties work relative to them. flex-basis sets the initial size of an item along the main axis, so it acts like width in a row and like height in a column.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The other values are row-reverse and column-reverse, which flip the order along the main axis.</li></ul>\n<h3>Container properties versus item properties</h3>\n<ul><li>display, gap and flex-direction go on the parent (the flex container).</li><li>flex-basis goes on the children (flex items); putting it on the container does nothing.</li><li>Because the divs in the exercise share no class or element type and the HTML must not change, the children are targeted with the child combinator and universal selector: .container &gt; *. The &gt; only matches direct children, not grandchildren or deeper descendants; * matches any element.</li><li>This lesson deliberately leaves flex sizing details (grow, shrink, what happens when there is not enough room) for a later dedicated lesson.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Column direction with sized items</p><pre data-lang=\"css\">.container {\n  display: inline-flex;\n  flex-direction: column;\n}\n\n.container &gt; * {\n  flex-basis: 100px;   /* height, because the main axis is vertical */\n}</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Same flex-basis in a row means width</p><pre data-lang=\"css\">.container { display: flex; flex-direction: row; }\n.container &gt; * { flex-basis: 100px; }  /* 100px wide */</pre>\n<h2>The task: Stack the rainbow vertically</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> index.html with a div.container (already display: flex, gold border, white text) holding seven coloured divs (Red to Purple) with different classes. HTML must not be modified. A goal image shows tall items stacked top to bottom.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Change the layout using CSS only so the colours stack vertically, each 100px tall, and the container is only as wide as its content.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Add flex-direction: column to .container so items run top to bottom.</li><li>Notice the items now stretch the full page width; change display: flex to display: inline-flex so the container shrinks to its widest content.</li><li>Try flex-basis: 100px on .container and observe that nothing changes, because it is an item property.</li><li>Use the docs (child combinator and universal selector) to write .container &gt; * and set flex-basis: 100px there.</li><li>Experiment: switch direction back to row, remove inline-flex, shrink the window, and compare.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Seven coloured blocks stacked vertically, each 100px high, inside a gold-bordered box that is only as wide as the longest label.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"css\">.container {\n  color: white;\n  border: 5px solid gold;\n  display: inline-flex;\n  flex-direction: column;\n}\n.container &gt; * {\n  flex-basis: 100px;\n}\n.red { background-color: #eb4d4b; }\n.orange { background-color: #f0932b; }\n.yellow { background-color: #f6e58d; }\n.green { background-color: #6ab04c; }\n.blue { background-color: #4834d4; }\n.indigo { background-color: #30336b; }\n.purple { background-color: #be2edd; }</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Putting flex-basis on the container.</li><li>Forgetting that flex-basis in a column controls height, not width.</li><li>Leaving display: flex so the column stretches the full page width.</li><li>Reference repo adds an author footer that is not part of the course.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l056", "<p class=\"summary\">Tour of the main flexbox layout properties, split into item-level (order, align-self) and container-level (flex-wrap, justify-content, align-items, align-content). Practised on a rainbow demo page and finished with the Flexbox Froggy game on intermediate difficulty.</p>\n<h3>Parent versus child properties</h3>\n<ul><li>Always ask whether a property belongs on the container (parent) or on the flex items (children). A child-only property placed on the container is ignored.</li><li>Container properties covered: flex-wrap, justify-content, align-items, align-content. Item properties covered: order, align-self.</li><li>A cheat sheet (CSS-Tricks flexbox guide) separates parent and child properties and lists every value; the advice is to look things up as needed rather than memorise, and revisit them when a project needs them.</li></ul>\n<h3>order (item property)</h3>\n<ul><li>Every flex item defaults to order: 0, so items follow HTML source order.</li><li>Giving one item a higher order (for example 1, 10 or 20) moves it after items with lower values; only the relative ranking matters, and ties fall back to source order.</li><li>Typical use: select one child (such as the green div) and set its order to push it to the end without editing the HTML. Negative values place items first.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> order changes visual order only; keyboard focus and screen readers still follow the HTML order, which can hurt accessibility.</li></ul>\n<h3>flex-wrap (container property)</h3>\n<ul><li>Default is nowrap (no hyphen): all items stay on one line and are squeezed until they can no longer shrink, after which they overflow off the page.</li><li>flex-wrap: wrap lets items that do not fit move to the next line, which makes layouts respond to narrower windows.</li><li>wrap-reverse also wraps but lines stack in the opposite cross direction (new lines go above rather than below).</li><li>Set on the container; it affects how all the children behave.</li></ul>\n<h3>justify-content (container, main axis)</h3>\n<ul><li>Distributes items along the main axis (horizontal for row, vertical for column, so it flips with flex-direction).</li><li>flex-start packs items at the start, flex-end at the end, center centres them. Centering this way is far simpler than older techniques.</li><li>space-between: first and last items touch the container edges with equal gaps between items.</li><li>space-around: equal space around each item, so gaps at the ends are half the size of gaps between items.</li><li>space-evenly: every gap, including the ends, is the same size.</li><li>Only has a visible effect when there is leftover room on the main axis.</li></ul>\n<h3>align-items (container, cross axis)</h3>\n<ul><li>Positions items along the cross axis (vertical for row).</li><li>The container needs extra height for this to be visible; by default it is only as tall as its tallest item. The demo sets height to 70vh (vh = percentage of the viewport height, 100vh is the full window).</li><li>Values: flex-start, flex-end, center, baseline and stretch (default).</li><li>Keep flex-wrap at nowrap while experimenting, because with wrapping on, multi-line behaviour is governed by align-content.</li></ul>\n<h3>align-self (item property)</h3>\n<ul><li>Overrides the container's align-items for one single item, e.g. centre all but make one item sit at the top.</li><li>Accepts the same family of values (flex-start, flex-end, center, etc.).</li></ul>\n<h3>align-content (container, wrapped lines)</h3>\n<ul><li>Like align-items but for the group of lines in a multi-line flex container; it only takes effect when flex-wrap is wrap (or wrap-reverse) and items have actually wrapped onto more than one line.</li><li>With nowrap it appears to do nothing. Values such as center, flex-start, flex-end control how the wrapped lines are spaced in the container.</li></ul>\n<h3>Practice resources</h3>\n<ul><li>A hosted flex-layout demo page (rainbow items with selectors for each property) for experimenting by resizing the window.</li><li>Flexbox Froggy: an interactive game where you write CSS to move a frog onto a lily pad. Task: complete all levels in Intermediate difficulty (settings menu), which hides the direction hints; switch to Beginner only if stuck.</li><li>Example from the demo level: with a row flex container, justify-content: flex-end moves the frog to the end.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Container-level layout properties</p><pre data-lang=\"css\">.container {\n  display: flex;\n  flex-wrap: wrap;                 /* nowrap (default) | wrap | wrap-reverse */\n  justify-content: space-between;  /* along the main axis */\n  align-items: center;             /* along the cross axis */\n  align-content: flex-start;       /* only matters when items wrap onto several lines */\n  height: 70vh;                    /* gives align-items room to work */\n}</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Item-level properties</p><pre data-lang=\"css\">.green { order: 10; }        /* pushed after the items that default to 0 */\n.red   { align-self: flex-start; } /* ignores the container's align-items */</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Centring a child in its parent</p><pre data-lang=\"css\">.parent {\n  display: flex;\n  justify-content: center;\n  align-items: center;\n}</pre>\n<h2>The task: Flexbox Froggy (intermediate mode)</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Online Flexbox Froggy game with a frog and lily pad per level and a CSS editor; difficulty set to Intermediate in settings.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Finish every level by writing flexbox CSS that moves the frog onto the lily pad, using the cheat sheet and what was learned in the lesson.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Open the game and choose Intermediate in settings (optionally language / colour-blind mode).</li><li>Read the level, inspect the starting display: flex rule, and decide which axis the frog has to move along.</li><li>Write container properties such as justify-content, align-items, flex-direction, flex-wrap, align-content, plus item properties such as order and align-self, as the levels demand.</li><li>Use the cheat sheet or demo page when stuck; drop to Beginner only if needed.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> All levels complete with the frog on the lily pad.</p>\n</div>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Using align-items for something that needs the container to be taller; without a set height there is no free cross-axis space.</li><li>Expecting align-content to work without flex-wrap: wrap.</li><li>Forgetting that justify-content / align-items swap meaning when flex-direction is column.</li><li>Putting order or align-self on the container.</li><li>Remember the unhyphenated nowrap value.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l057", "<p class=\"summary\">Explains how a flex container works out the size of each flex item: a priority list of size sources, the min/max limits, and the flex-grow, flex-shrink and flex-basis properties with the flex shorthand. Ends with an online exercise where you reproduce the resize behaviour of a target flex container.</p>\n<h3>Default behaviour of flex items</h3>\n<ul><li>Paragraphs are normally full-width block elements. Once their parent is display: flex they become flex items laid out along the main axis (a row by default), each sized to its content with no gaps unless gap is set.</li><li>With no sizing properties at all, items already respond to window resizing: they shrink with the container down to a minimum size, then overflow off the page.</li><li>The default minimum size of an item is the width of its longest word (text will wrap but a word is not broken). The default ideal/maximum size is all of its text on one line. So the item with the longest single word stays widest when squeezed.</li></ul>\n<h3>Priority list used to pick an item's size</h3>\n<ul><li>1. min-width / max-width (or min-height / max-height in a column) act as hard limits and win over everything else.</li><li>2. flex-basis, if set. It sets the starting size along the main axis (width in a row, height in a column).</li><li>3. width (or height in a column container), if there is no flex-basis.</li><li>4. Otherwise the natural content size.</li><li>Because flex-basis outranks width, setting both is pointless: the width is ignored in a row container.</li></ul>\n<h3>width and flex-basis in practice</h3>\n<ul><li>Setting width: 100px on each of four items gives four 100px boxes while the container is at least 400px wide (plus any gaps).</li><li>When the container gets narrower than the total, the items are still allowed to shrink (flex-shrink defaults to 1), but never below their minimum content size, so the longest word stays intact.</li><li>Setting flex-basis: 200px makes each item aim for 200px and overrides the width. It shrinks the same way once space runs out.</li></ul>\n<h3>max-width and min-width</h3>\n<ul><li>max-width caps how large an item may become. If flex-basis is 200px but max-width is 100px, the item ends at 100px even with plenty of room. If flex-basis is smaller than max-width (e.g. 50px), the basis is used.</li><li>max-width therefore limits growth potential; items still shrink and grow with the container but only within the cap.</li><li>min-width sets how small an item may shrink. If min-width (300px) is larger than flex-basis (200px), the item renders at 300px and will not shrink below it.</li><li>Both properties replace the content-derived defaults if you set them explicitly, which is sometimes useful.</li></ul>\n<h3>flex-grow and flex-shrink</h3>\n<ul><li>These provide the 'flexible' part of flexbox. flex-grow says whether an item may expand into spare space; flex-shrink says whether it may contract when space is short. Value 0 turns each off.</li><li>grow 0 and shrink 0: items stay fixed at the basis size whatever the window does. It stops behaving like a flexible layout.</li><li>grow 1 and shrink 0: items start at the basis, expand to fill the container when it widens, but do not shrink under the basis (they overflow instead).</li><li>grow 0 and shrink 1: this is the default for flex items. Items begin at their basis, do not stretch, but can shrink down to their minimum content width.</li><li>grow 1 and shrink 1: items expand to fill the container and also shrink; the basis is effectively just a starting point, and the limits come from max/min-width or content.</li><li>The default flex-basis is auto, which uses the width if set, else the content size, so items with more text get a bigger share. Setting flex-basis: 0 removes this and makes shares equal.</li></ul>\n<h3>The flex shorthand</h3>\n<ul><li>flex: &lt;grow&gt; &lt;shrink&gt; &lt;basis&gt;, in that order, e.g. flex: 1 1 0.</li><li>A single unitless number such as flex: 1 means grow 1, shrink 1, basis 0, so every item gets an equal share.</li><li>Different numbers set proportions: an item with flex: 2 and another with flex: 1 split the space 2:1 and keep that ratio as the container is resized. This is how the earlier section's red/blue boxes with different flex values worked.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Four items that keep a 100px width, shrinking only when needed</p><pre data-lang=\"css\">.container {\n  display: flex;\n  gap: 10px;\n}\n.container p {\n  width: 100px;   /* used only if no flex-basis */\n}</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">flex-basis beats width; max-width caps it; min-width raises it</p><pre data-lang=\"css\">.item {\n  width: 100px;        /* ignored */\n  flex-basis: 200px;   /* starting size */\n  max-width: 150px;    /* final size cannot exceed this */\n  min-width: 50px;     /* cannot shrink below this */\n}</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Longhand versus shorthand</p><pre data-lang=\"css\">.item {\n  flex-grow: 1;\n  flex-shrink: 1;\n  flex-basis: 0;\n}\n/* same as */\n.item { flex: 1 1 0; }\n/* same as */\n.item { flex: 1; }</pre>\n<h2>The task: Match the resize behaviour of the green flex box</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> An external exercise page with a CSS text box and an 'Apply CSS' button. A green flex container (the target) and a blue container (yours) hold three items. Target widths are listed on screen: item 1 200px, item 2 200px, item 3 400px.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Write CSS so the blue container resizes exactly like the green one, which means matching both the static layout and the behaviour while the window is resized.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Set justify-content: space-between on the container to match how the items are spread.</li><li>Give the items flex-basis values of 200px, 200px and 400px.</li><li>Resize the window and watch the green box: only item 1 shrinks, items 2 and 3 hold their size.</li><li>Set flex-shrink: 0 on item 2 and item 3, leaving item 1 on its default shrink of 1.</li><li>Press Apply CSS and re-test by resizing.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Blue items look identical to the green ones at full width and respond identically when the window is narrowed.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"css\">.container {\n  justify-content: space-between;\n}\n.item1 { flex-basis: 200px; }\n.item2 { flex-basis: 200px; flex-shrink: 0; }\n.item3 { flex-basis: 400px; flex-shrink: 0; }</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Checking only the static layout: matching widths is not enough, you must resize to compare grow/shrink behaviour.</li><li>Setting width and flex-basis together; the width is ignored in a row container.</li><li>Expecting a flex-basis larger than max-width to win. The max-width limit wins.</li><li>Setting both grow and shrink to 0 and wondering why the layout no longer responds.</li><li>Leaving flex-basis on auto when you want equal item sizes; use flex: 1 (basis 0).</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The selectors in the solution are illustrative; the exercise page uses its own class names.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("s09-pricing", "<p class=\"summary\">Section project: build a three-tier pricing table with flexbox so the cards share the width on large screens and stack vertically on small ones. The lecture sets the brief, then walks through the solution CSS block by block.</p>\n<h3>Project brief</h3>\n<ul><li>Starting files: index.html with all the HTML content already written, plus goal-large and goal-small images. The only CSS supplied is the Google font Sono on the body.</li><li>Required: cards live in a flex container, adapt to the available width, and stack one above another on narrow screens. Colours, radius, borders and other visual choices are free.</li><li>Hint given: combine a media query with flexbox to get the stacking behaviour.</li></ul>\n<h3>Solution: the container</h3>\n<ul><li>display: flex turns the three cards into flex items; gap puts space between them (2rem in the solution).</li><li>justify-content: center and align-items: center plus height: 100vh centres the cards horizontally and vertically in the viewport. This is a quick way to centre content in both directions, which is awkward without flexbox or grid.</li></ul>\n<h3>Solution: the cards</h3>\n<ul><li>flex: 1 gives each card an equal share that can grow and shrink.</li><li>max-width: 400px stops cards from stretching across the whole wide screen, which would defeat the centring.</li><li>Supporting styles: padding 20px, light grey background, border-radius 5px, text-align: center.</li></ul>\n<h3>Solution: text, list and button styling</h3>\n<ul><li>.plan-title: larger font-size (24px), bold, small margin-bottom.</li><li>.plan-price: much larger (48px), bold, margin-bottom so it stands out.</li><li>.plan-features: list-style: none removes bullets from the ul; padding: 0 and margin: 0 remove default indentation; each li gets a margin-bottom to space rows and push the button down.</li><li>.plan-button: orange background, white text, padding, rounded corners, border: none to drop the default grey border.</li></ul>\n<h3>Solution: responsive stacking with a media query</h3>\n<ul><li>@media (max-width: 1250px) targets the container and switches flex-direction from row to column, so cards stack.</li><li>Height is changed to 100% in the same block. A fixed 100vh would cut off the extra content once cards are stacked and the content becomes taller than the viewport; 100% lets the container grow with its content.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Core flexbox and responsive rules (from the reference repo, shortened)</p><pre data-lang=\"css\">.pricing-container {\n  display: flex;\n  justify-content: center;\n  align-items: center;\n  height: 100vh;\n  gap: 2rem;\n}\n\n.pricing-plan {\n  flex: 1;\n  max-width: 400px;\n  padding: 20px;\n  background-color: #f2f2f2;\n  border-radius: 5px;\n  text-align: center;\n}\n\n.plan-features {\n  list-style: none;\n  padding: 0;\n  margin: 0;\n}\n\n.plan-button {\n  background-color: #ff6600;\n  color: white;\n  padding: 10px;\n  border: none;\n  border-radius: 5px;\n}\n\n@media (max-width: 1250px) {\n  .pricing-container {\n    flex-direction: column;\n    height: 100%;\n  }\n}</pre>\n<h2>The task: Flexbox Pricing Table project</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> index.html with HTML for three plans (.pricing-plan cards with .plan-title, .plan-price, .plan-features list and .plan-button) and only the Sono font applied. goal-large.png and goal-small.png show targets.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Style the page so three cards sit in a flex container, share width evenly, are centred, and stack vertically below a width breakpoint.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Make .pricing-container a flex container with a gap.</li><li>Centre the cards with justify-content and align-items, and give the container a viewport-height height.</li><li>Give .pricing-plan flex: 1 and a max-width.</li><li>Style the card (padding, background, radius, centred text), the title and price, the feature list and the button.</li><li>Add a media query that switches the container to flex-direction: column and releases the fixed height.</li><li>Resize the window to confirm both layouts and compare with the goal images.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Three equal cards in a centred row on wide screens, a single stacked column on narrow screens, no clipped content when scrolling.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"css\">.pricing-container {\n  display: flex;\n  justify-content: center;\n  align-items: center;\n  height: 100vh;\n  gap: 2rem;\n}\n.pricing-plan {\n  flex: 1;\n  max-width: 400px;\n  padding: 20px;\n  background-color: #f2f2f2;\n  border-radius: 5px;\n  text-align: center;\n}\n@media (max-width: 1250px) {\n  .pricing-container {\n    flex-direction: column;\n    height: 100%;\n  }\n}</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Keeping height: 100vh in the stacked layout, which clips content.</li><li>Omitting max-width so cards span the entire wide screen.</li><li>Forgetting border: none on the button, leaving the default border.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The repo solution.html contains an author footer ('Made with ...') that should be ignored; solution.html and index.html in the repo also differ only in property order and values like prices.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Media queries with max-width apply at and below the width given; 1250px is a choice, not a rule.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l059", "<p class=\"summary\">A short motivational tip on habit building: attach the new coding habit to an existing routine so the old habit acts as a trigger.</p>\n<h3>Habit stacking with a cue</h3>\n<ul><li>Pick a habit you already do reliably (for example brushing teeth) and perform the new habit straight after it.</li><li>The existing routine becomes the trigger, so you do not rely on remembering or on willpower.</li><li>Applied to coding: choose a fixed daily cue, then do a set block (about 30-60 minutes) of coding right after it.</li><li>Keep the new habit specific and small enough to start immediately, with nothing else slotted in between.</li></ul>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Leaving the habit unanchored so something else always gets in the way.</li></ul>");
